= Conclusion <sec:conclusion>

This paper demonstrates that a unikernel can support practical graphics display workloads without importing a full Linux graphics subsystem. VOGUE implements a standards-based VirtIO-GPU 2D pipeline, a minimal EGL/GLES2/GBM/DRM shim, and a CPU software rasterizer as Unikraft micro-libraries. It also implements the real VirtIO-GPU 3D/blob control-queue surface needed for the next accelerated stage while preserving Unikraft's design principle of reusing existing PCI, virtio, allocator, and POSIX/mmap support instead of reimplementing them.

Our evaluation validates progressive milestones: (disp.2d) a correct 2D display pipeline verified by native evidence-gated tests; (gfx.kmscube.sw) upstream _kmscube_ source compiled and running against the shim with software rendering; (gfx.glmark2.sw) an additional application framework demonstrating substrate generality; (proto.real-driver, vk.readiness) real-driver ABI/readiness and benchmark-design gates; and (vk.drm-shim, gfx.vkmark, vk.ggml-dispatch) Venus/Vulkan and ggml substrate gates. The current generated matrix keeps `xport.qemu-vgpu` as `blocked:probe-incomplete` and K1 `SUBMIT_3D` / pixel-correct frame evidence as `blocked:missing-pass-marker`; native virgl encoder and Venus protocol tests are readiness evidence, not same-run host-rendering proof. The remaining gap is to rebuild and rerun the appliance under a QEMU GL/Venus environment and capture row-compatible runtime artifacts.

The accelerated rendering path remains the most significant remaining milestone. The infrastructure for it — context management, capability queries, blob resources, UUID/map/unmap, 3D submission, Venus bootstrap command serialization with verified wire-format correctness (proto.venus-enc: PASS; PACKED encoding, uint32 array_size per Mesa protocol), and the full Mesa ring-buffer protocol (proto.venus-ring: PASS for native checks) — is in place as substrate evidence. The next concrete step is to turn this substrate into same-run QEMU/Venus transport, K1 frame, and ggml/Venus compute-offload evidence without overclaiming rows that still lack fresh runtime proof. We believe the progressive validation approach and evidence-gated evaluation framework described in this paper provide a sound methodology for reaching that milestone without overclaiming along the way.
